# Line endings, pinned per extension and on purpose.
#
# Git for Windows installs with `core.autocrlf=true`, so `actions/checkout`
# rewrites the working tree to CRLF there. That is not cosmetic: `gofmt`
# treats a CRLF file as unformatted, and CI's "Go plugin tests" step failed
# on all 37 files under plugins/go the first time a Windows job got far
# enough to reach it (GM-334). Pinning the sources to LF fixes every clone,
# not only CI - a Windows contributor running `gofmt` locally hit the same
# wall with nothing to explain it.
#
# Deliberately NOT `* text=auto eol=lf`. `core/tests/fixtures/*.rpc` are
# stored WITH CRLF on purpose - they are JSON-RPC control-plane captures and
# Content-Length framing is \r\n-delimited - and that directory's own
# .gitattributes marks them `-text`. Those four are the only tracked files in
# the repository that contain a CR byte at all; that was checked rather than
# assumed, which is what makes every rule below a no-op on content. The sole
# behaviour change is what a Windows checkout writes. Keep it that way: a
# blanket root rule is one edit away from fighting the fixtures.
#
# `*.ps1` is absent on purpose. scripts/install.ps1 reaches its users through
# `irm ... | iex`, which fetches the blob (LF) rather than a checkout, so the
# attribute would not touch the path that matters - and nothing lints or runs
# it from a checkout on any runner. A Windows contributor reading it locally
# gets CRLF, which is what PowerShell tooling expects.

# --- sources ---------------------------------------------------------------
# gofmt is the one that actually breaks today. The rest are pinned before
# they do: every formatter and every byte offset in the index is computed
# from these files.
*.rs            text eol=lf
*.go            text eol=lf
*.ts            text eol=lf
*.js            text eol=lf
*.cjs           text eol=lf
*.py            text eol=lf
*.pyi           text eol=lf

# --- shell -----------------------------------------------------------------
# A CRLF .sh is a latent bug, not a style question: Git Bash executes
# scripts/*.sh on the Windows runner, and a trailing \r turns a heredoc
# terminator or a `case` pattern into something that no longer matches.
*.sh            text eol=lf

# --- manifests, lockfiles, workflows ---------------------------------------
*.toml          text eol=lf
*.lock          text eol=lf
*.json          text eol=lf
*.yml           text eol=lf
*.mod           text eol=lf
*.sum           text eol=lf
*.work          text eol=lf

# --- test fixtures ---------------------------------------------------------
# NOT *.rpc - see the header. These three are read as text and their line
# endings are part of what the extractors and the bulk plane parse.
*.ndjson        text eol=lf
*.toy           text eol=lf
*.fk            text eol=lf

# --- docs and repository metadata ------------------------------------------
*.md            text eol=lf
*.html          text eol=lf
.gitignore      text eol=lf
.gitattributes  text eol=lf
